{"name":"Stargazer","hex":"#3f5865","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stargazer","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone who gazes at the stars; an astronomer or astrologer (now especially an amateur one). .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Any member of the family Uranoscopidae of bottom-dwelling fish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stargazer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Observational astronomy","blocks":[{"text":"Observational astronomy is a division of astronomy that is concerned with recording data about the observable universe, the practice and study of observing celestial objects with the use of telescopes and other astronomical instruments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Mayall_Telescope.jpg/330px-Mayall_Telescope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Observational_astronomy","wikidata":"Q818557"},{"title":"Stargazer (aircraft)","blocks":[{"text":"Stargazer is a Lockheed L-1011 TriStar built in 1974 and modified in 1994 by Orbital Sciences to serve as the mother ship for the Pegasus, an air-launched orbital rocket. As of June 2021, it has conducted 39 Pegasus launches carrying nearly 100 satellites. It is the only airworthy L-1011 remaining in service as of 2026 because its FAA-approved launch license was valid until March 9, 2026. It received an \"Emergency Special Permit Authorization\" on March 20, 2026 for \"immediate national security purposes\" to enable the Swift boost mission launch."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/U.S._Space_Force_successfully_launches_first_tactically_responsive_launch_mission_210613-F-XXZZZ-0001.jpg/330px-U.S._Space_Force_successfully_launches_first_tactically_responsive_launch_mission_210613-F-XXZZZ-0001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stargazer_(aircraft)","wikidata":"Q6133784"},{"title":"Stargazer (fish)","blocks":[{"text":"The stargazers are a family, Uranoscopidae, of perciform ray-finned fish that have eyes on top of their heads. The family includes about 51 species in eight genera, all marine and found worldwide in shallow and deep saltwaters."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/FMIB_45714_Kathetostoma_laeve.jpeg/330px-FMIB_45714_Kathetostoma_laeve.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stargazer_(fish)","wikidata":"Q183280"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Astronoomiahuvilised.jpg/500px-Astronoomiahuvilised.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Astronoomiahuvilised.jpg/1280px-Astronoomiahuvilised.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Astronoomiahuvilised.jpg/500px-Astronoomiahuvilised.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An assembly in Estonia to observe meteors","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAstronoomiahuvilised.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Crab_Nebula_in_Multiple_Wavelengths.png/500px-Crab_Nebula_in_Multiple_Wavelengths.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Crab_Nebula_in_Multiple_Wavelengths.png/1280px-Crab_Nebula_in_Multiple_Wavelengths.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Crab_Nebula_in_Multiple_Wavelengths.png/500px-Crab_Nebula_in_Multiple_Wavelengths.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Crab Nebula 